The challenge request never reaches the token file. HTTP-01 validation requests the token over plain HTTP at that exact path. Your blanket redirect catches /.well-known too and sends the CA to the HTTPS vhost - which serves the SPA from a different document root, where the token does not exist, hence the 404 the CA reports. The main domain renews because its vhost has no such trap.

Fix: exempt the challenge path before the redirect, in both the HTTP and HTTPS blocks:

location ^~ /.well-known/acme-challenge/ {
    root /var/www/acme;
    try_files $uri =404;
}

and point certbot's webroot at /var/www/acme for this domain. The alternative that sidesteps web config entirely is the DNS-01 challenge - worth it if this vhost's routing keeps changing.

TLS terminates before nginx can route by hostname, and for port 443 you effectively have only one server block. When a client connects to 443 with SNI shop.example.com, nginx looks for a 443 listener whose server_name matches. The shop block never declares one - it only listens on 80 - so the connection falls to the block marked default_server for 443, which is blog. The client therefore gets blog's certificate (and blog's content) no matter what name it asked for. The redirect in the shop block never runs, because the request never reaches HTTP on that block.

Fix: give the shop block its own TLS listener and certificate:

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name shop.example.com;
    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/shop.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/shop.example.com/privkey.pem;
    ...
}

Confirm the shop cert's SANs actually cover shop.example.com, then test with openssl s_client -servername shop.example.com -connect <ip>:443 and check the returned CN. Also consider making the default_server a throwaway block with a dummy cert that returns 444, so future misconfigurations fail loudly instead of serving the wrong site.

Our deploy script fails when run as ssh host 'deploy.sh' with "node: command not found", but if I SSH in interactively and run the same script it works. The top of ~/.bashrc has the standard guard:

case $- in
  *i*) ;;
  *) return;;
esac

and the nvm setup that puts node on PATH is below it.

